  "pinNumbering": {
    "value": "rows a, b, c x pins 1-32; the drawing prints a pin as 'B C14' = plug B, row c, pin 14",
    "source": "SCH",
    "page": "sheet 3",
    "confidence": "read"
  },
  "note": "Plug B is card specific: the backplane wires it per slot, unlike plug C. Sheet 3 draws every plug B pin, all as six-sided (both ways) ports, fed from the bus named OSCSI; the signal names are SCSI lines, which confirms pcb.json's 'B = SCSI bus'. Pattern on the drawing: the plain signal is on row c, the copy with a tilde on rows a and b joined together. OSCSI comes from sheet 18 (part 18A, 'SING_DIFF_SW'), which switches the lines between single-ended and differential SCSI; which pins carry what in each mode is read from that sheet, not here. Pins 26-32 of every row are not drawn. Glyphs settled by shape: zero has an inner stroke, letter O has none; 5 has a flat top, S a curved top; 8 is closed.",
  "unusedPins": {
    "value": [
      "row a: 26-32",
      "row b: 26-32",
      "row c: 26-32"
    ],
    "source": "SCH",
    "page": "sheet 3",
    "confidence": "read"
  },
  "disagreements": {
    "value": "Plug B on 3207 draws 75 positions, rows a, b and c pins 1-25, with SCSI signals on row c. ND-06.016.01 section I.2.3 (1980) says plugs A and B have 64 lines each (recorded in nd100-c-connector). The drawing is taken as the truth for this card (owner ruling 2026-11-27). Whether the 1980 figure is wrong, or later backplanes wire more of plugs A and B, is open: ND-06.017.01 'ND-100 Backplane/Bus Description' (1979) would settle it but no copy has been found.",
    "source": "SCH",
    "page": "sheets 1-3",
    "confidence": "open"
  },
